{"id":"muhammad-akif-janjua/linear-guard","name":"Linear Guard","version":"1.5.4","publisher_pubkey":"e469d55383447fc6b95cbffb786fee7cc5c998c9dbe3143e7eec8ad210524540","provider":"linear","auth":{"type":"api_key","vault_provider":"linear","header":"Authorization","secret_field":"LINEAR_API_KEY","docs":"https://linear.app/settings/api"},"allowed_destinations":[],"description":"Linear Guard is a governance-first Linear integration for engineering, product, and operations teams that want AI-assisted issue management without silent or unrestricted changes.\n\nIt provides 16 focused commands for the real Linear API: identify the current user; list teams, projects, labels, workflow states, workspace members, and team cycles; search and retrieve issues; analyze sprint health; create and update issues; apply a complete governed triage decision; plan a bounded sprint; rebalance 2–5 existing sprint issues through one batch update; and add comments.\n\nThe ten discovery, reporting, and read commands are low-risk. The six commands that change Linear use RailCall's preview → approve → execute flow, and Linear is not changed until a human approves the exact payload.\n\nThe flagship linear.plan_sprint command creates 2–5 fully configured issues through Linear's server-side issueBatchCreate transaction. One approval can bind the whole issue set to a team and cycle, optionally link every issue to a project, workflow state, or parent issue, and apply per-issue assignees, labels, priorities, estimates, titles, and descriptions. Every referenced entity is preflighted before the single write request. The blast radius is bounded and summarized in the signed receipt.\n\nlinear.triage_issue provides a second governed composite for changing priority, workflow state, assignee, project, cycle, labels, and an optional audit note under one approval.\n\nlinear.rebalance_sprint closes the loop between sprint diagnosis and action: it preflights 2–5 same-team issues, detects whether the requested shared change is a no-op, and applies one bounded priority, estimate, state, assignee, project, cycle, or label-set decision through Linear's issueBatchUpdate mutation and a single RailCall approval.\n\nLinear Guard uses the real Linear GraphQL API with no mocks or stubs. It checks GraphQL errors even when the HTTP response is 200, reports authentication and rate-limit failures clearly, never automatically retries writes, and does not present failed operations as successful results.\n\nQuick start: install Linear Guard, configure a Linear personal API key through RailCall Studio → Integrations → Linear, reload Modules, and begin with linear.get_current_user or linear.list_teams. Preview every write before approving it.\n\nKnown limitations: personal API keys operate with the permissions of the Linear user who created them. Search results and multi-record outputs are bounded to remain readable in RailCall receipts.\n\nBuilt for the RailCall Track A module contest.\n\ncontest:2026Q3\n\nLinear Guard declares zero model-provider destinations in its signed manifest. Its Linear GraphQL traffic is the module's business integration path, not an LLM call, and the handler does not invoke any model-provider SDK or RailCall model-completion primitive.","commands":[{"id":"linear.get_current_user","title":"Get Current Linear User","provider":"linear","risk":"low","mode":"read","requires":[],"preview":true,"receipt_required":true,"input_schema":{},"output_schema":{"user_id":"string","name":"string","email":"string","http_status":"number"}},{"id":"linear.list_teams","title":"List Linear Teams","provider":"linear","risk":"low","mode":"read","requires":[],"preview":true,"receipt_required":true,"input_schema":{},"output_schema":{"team_count":"number","teams_json":"string","http_status":"number"}},{"id":"linear.list_projects","title":"List Linear Projects","provider":"linear","risk":"low","mode":"read","requires":[],"preview":true,"receipt_required":true,"input_schema":{},"output_schema